.TH FLOCK 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
flock \- run a program while holding a lock on a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flock
.RB [ \-sxn ]
.I file
.I prog
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B flock
.RB [ \-sxn ]
.I file
.B \-c
.I command
.br
.B flock
.RB [ \-sxun ]
.I fd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flock
opens
.IR file ,
locks it, runs
.I prog
with the arguments
.IR arg ...,
waits for it, and exits with its exit status.
The lock is released when
.B flock
exits.
Two
.B flock
commands that lock the same file therefore run their programs one at a
time, which serializes jobs that must not overlap.
If
.I file
does not exist,
.B flock
creates it as an empty file; see
.B Lock range
below.
.I file
is opened for reading only, and
.B flock
never writes to it.
This
.B flock
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A lock is exclusive or shared.
An exclusive lock, the default, conflicts with every other lock on the
file.
A shared lock, taken with
.BR \-s ,
conflicts only with exclusive locks, so several shared locks may be
held at once.
When the lock conflicts with one held by another process,
.B flock
waits until it is released, or with
.B \-n
fails at once.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
.B flock
runs
.I command
with the shell, as
.I shell
.B \-c
.IR command ,
where
.I shell
is the value of
.B SHELL
or, when that is unset or empty,
.IR /bin/sh ,
which is the kit's
.BR sh (1).
.PP
With a single operand, a file descriptor number
.IR fd ,
.B flock
locks or unlocks the file already open on that descriptor and exits.
On Windows this form cannot hold a lock beyond the life of
.B flock
itself; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
.I prog
is started as the kit's shell starts an external command: an applet of
that name runs as a new busybox process, or inside the
.B flock
process for the few applets that busybox runs that way, such as
.BR echo ,
and any other name without a directory part is looked up in
.BR PATH ;
see
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1).
.I prog
shares the standard input, output, and error of
.BR flock ,
which writes nothing itself except error messages on standard error.
.SS Lock range
.B flock
locks the bytes of
.I file
that exist when the lock is taken, from offset 0 up to the size of the
file at that moment.
Bytes appended later are not covered.
A lock on an empty file covers no bytes, and Windows does not let a lock
of zero bytes conflict with any other lock.
A lock on an empty file therefore excludes nothing, and two
.B flock
commands on it run their programs at the same time.
Write at least one byte to a lock file before using it, as in
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
Windows locks are mandatory, not advisory.
While
.B flock
holds an exclusive lock, no other process, including
.IR prog ,
can read or write the locked bytes of
.IR file .
While it holds a shared lock, no process can write them.
Such a read or write fails with a permission error.
Use a separate file as the lock, not the data that
.I prog
works on.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only before
.I file
or
.IR fd ,
and may be grouped, as in
.BR \-sn .
Everything after
.I file
belongs to
.IR prog ,
except a
.B \-c
or
.B \-\-command
immediately after
.IR file .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-shared
Take a shared lock.
Incompatible with
.B \-x
and
.BR \-u .
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exclusive
Take an exclusive lock.
This is the default.
Incompatible with
.B \-s
and
.BR \-u .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unlock
Release a lock instead of taking one.
A lock belongs to the process that took it, and a new
.B flock
process holds none, so
.B \-u
always fails with status 1, and
.I prog
does not run.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nonblock
Do not wait for a conflicting lock to be released.
Exit at once with status 1 and an error message instead.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR \fIcommand\fR
Run
.I command
with the shell, as described above.
It must follow
.I file
directly, and
.I command
must be a single argument.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell that runs
.I command
for
.BR \-c ,
when set and not empty.
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.I prog
is looked up when it is not an applet and has no directory part.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that are not run when named as
.IR prog ,
so that a program in
.B PATH
runs instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments of
.B flock
are expanded when it starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
That includes the arguments of
.IR prog .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.I n
.I prog
ran and exited with status
.IR n .
A
.I prog
that ended as if by a signal gives 128 plus the signal number.
.TP
.B 0
In the
.I fd
form, the lock was taken.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, a
.I file
that could not be opened, a lock that could not be taken or released,
or, with
.BR \-n ,
a conflicting lock.
.I prog
does not run.
.TP
.B 255
.I prog
could not be started.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
flock: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
flock: : \fIreason\fR
flock: invalid number \(aq\fIfd\fR\(aq
flock: \-c takes only one argument
flock: \fIprog\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
The second form, with an empty field before the reason, reports a
failure to lock or unlock, such as
.B Permission denied
for a conflicting lock with
.BR \-n ,
.B Invalid argument
for
.B \-s
with
.BR \-x ,
or
.B Bad file descriptor
for an
.I fd
that is not open.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a lock file of one line, then run a build that no other build
using the same lock file can overlap:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo lock >build.lock
$ flock build.lock make
.EE
.RE
.PP
Skip the build when another one holds the lock:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ flock \-n build.lock make || echo busy
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a shell command under a shared lock:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ flock \-s build.lock \-c \(aqcp out/app.exe /c/dist/\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from
.B flock
on Unix:
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix a lock belongs to an open file and outlives the process that
took it while the file stays open elsewhere, so
.B "flock 9"
can lock a file that a shell opened with
.B "exec 9>file"
and hold the lock for the rest of the script.
On Windows a lock belongs to the process that took it and is released
when that process exits.
In the
.I fd
form
.B flock
takes the lock and releases it as it exits, so the shell never holds
it.
The only use of this form is to wait until no other process holds a
conflicting lock.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-u
always fails, as described under
.BR OPTIONS .
.IP \(bu 2
Locks are mandatory, and a lock on an empty file excludes nothing; see
.BR "Lock range" .
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-n ,
a conflicting lock gives an error message as well as status 1.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory cannot be opened, so it cannot be locked.
.B flock
exits with status 1 and
.BR "Permission denied" .
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-E ,
or
.B \-F
options, and no timeout.
.PP
Windows does not let another process delete or rename
.I file
while
.B flock
has it open.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR timeout (1)
